{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ByozmxjmLuwTHzqi8p6YF5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Experiment Plan — demo_calibrated_sim\n2\t# Target: EMNLP 2026 Findings (ARR deadline 2026-05-25)\n3\t# Created: 2026-05-13 (post-MVP, D013/D014)\n4\t# Story: Calibration failure taxonomy + scale-dependent failure severity\n5\t\n6\tclaims:\n7\t  C1:\n8\t    statement: \"BCP produces three systematic failure mechanisms (FM1-FM3) at 7B scale\"\n9\t    evidence_strength: moderate\n10\t    notes: \"FM1/FM3 are capacity-dependent (7B only); FM2 persists weakly at 14B\"\n11\t  C2:\n12\t    statement: \"DPO+BCP interaction produces calibration reversal (FM4)\"\n13\t    evidence_strength: strong\n14\t    notes: \"4 runs × 20 tasks, 10/12 variant×run gap expansion\"\n15\t  C3:\n16\t    statement: \"BCP failure severity is scale-dependent — catastrophic at 7B, selective ignoring at 14B\"\n17\t    evidence_strength: strong\n18\t    notes: \"Safety-efficacy gap: scaling resolves harms but not ineffectiveness\"\n19\t  C4:\n20\t    statement: \"Actionable implications for future calibration research\"\n21\t    evidence_strength: analytical\n22\t    notes: \"Grounded in C1-C3 data; 5 implications including scale-dependency\"\n23\t\n24\tplans:\n25\t  # --- P0: Core experiments (all completed) ---\n26\t\n27\t  - plan_id: plan_001\n28\t    name: \"7B BCP Baseline Evaluation\"\n29\t    claim: C1\n30\t    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct BCP eval on tau-bench retail (6 variants × 19-20 tasks). Documents FM1 (completion collapse), FM2 (cross-dim interference), FM3 (overcorrection rebound at single-run level).\"\n31\t    priority: P0\n32\t    depends_on: []\n33\t    registry_id: mvp-004\n34\t    status: done\n35\t\n36\t  - plan_id: plan_002\n37\t    name: \"7B DPO Training (v3, Fix B)\"\n38\t    claim: C2\n39\t    description: \"DPO fine-tuning on dialect-calibrated preference pairs. Training acc 0.75-1.0, loss 0.653.\"\n40\t    priority: P0\n41\t    depends_on: []\n42\t    registry_id: dpo-003\n43\t    status: done\n44\t\n45\t  - plan_id: plan_003\n46\t    name: \"7B DPO+BCP Evaluation (merged model)\"\n47\t    claim: [C1, C2]\n48\t    description: \"Evaluate DPO-trained merged model with BCP prompts (4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes). Confirms FM3 rebound (4/4 runs). Documents FM4 reversal (10/12 gap expansion).\"\n49\t    priority: P0\n50\t    depends_on: [plan_002]\n51\t    registry_id: hgate-dpo003-merged\n52\t    status: done\n53\t\n54\t  - plan_id: plan_004\n55\t    name: \"14B BCP Cross-Scale Evaluation\"\n56\t    claim: C3\n57\t    description: \"Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct FP16 TP=2 BCP eval (4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes). Result: FM1 NOT replicated (H3 4/4 PASS), FM3 NOT replicated (0/4 runs), FM2 weak. BCP still fails (H2 3/4 FAIL) but via 'selective ignoring' not 'catastrophic collapse'.\"\n58\t    priority: P0\n59\t    depends_on: [plan_001]\n60\t    registry_id: qwen14b-bcp-001\n61\t    status: done\n62\t\n63\t  # --- P1: Statistical robustness (no GPU needed) ---\n64\t\n65\t  - plan_id: plan_005\n66\t    name: \"Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons\"\n67\t    claim: [C1, C2, C3]\n68\t    description: \"Bootstrap confidence intervals for: FM4 reversal direction, FM3 rebound magnitude, 7B-vs-14B completion difference, H2 calibration gap. Computational only.\"\n69\t    priority: P1\n70\t    depends_on: [plan_001, plan_003, plan_004]\n71\t    registry_id: null\n72\t    gpu_estimate: \"0h (CPU only)\"\n73\t    status: planned\n74\t\n75\t  # --- P2: Optional strengthening ---\n76\t\n77\t  - plan_id: plan_006\n78\t    name: \"14B DPO + BCP (scale-dependent FM4?)\"\n79\t    claim: [C2, C3]\n80\t    description: \"DPO training + BCP eval at 14B scale. Tests whether FM4 (DPO+BCP reversal) is also scale-dependent. If 14B DPO+BCP shows no reversal → FM4 is capacity-dependent too. If reversal persists → FM4 is the one truly structural failure.\"\n81\t    priority: P2\n82\t    depends_on: [plan_004]\n83\t    registry_id: null\n84\t    gpu_estimate: \"~11-13h on 2× RTX 4090 (6-8h training + 5h eval)\"\n85\t    status: planned\n86\t    notes: \"Not strictly necessary — C2 already strong at 7B. Interesting for story depth. Contingent on new server GPU availability post-migration.\"\n87\t\n88\t  # --- Auxiliary experiments (supporting data, not primary evidence) ---\n89\t\n90\t  - plan_id: plan_aux_001\n91\t    name: \"Llama-3-8B BCP (floor effect)\"\n92\t    claim: C3\n93\t    description: \"Llama-3-8B completion=5%. Tool-calling insufficient for 16-tool tau-bench. Cannot isolate BCP effect.\"\n94\t    priority: null\n95\t    registry_id: llama-bcp-001\n96\t    status: done\n97\t    notes: \"Not usable for cross-model generalization. Documented in limitations.\"\n98\t\n99\t  - plan_id: plan_aux_002\n100\t    name: \"Mistral-7B BCP (floor effect)\"\n101\t    claim: C3\n102\t    description: \"Mistral-7B completion=0%. Cannot do structured tool_calls. No usable data.\"\n103\t    priority: null\n104\t    registry_id: mistral-bcp-001\n105\t    status: done\n106\t    notes: \"Confirms 16-tool setting is challenging for 7B non-Qwen models.\"\n107\t\n108\t  - plan_id: plan_aux_003\n109\t    name: \"LoRA Merge Diagnostics\"\n110\t    claim: C2\n111\t    description: \"Verified LoRA merge correctness (merged 60% vs base 40% completion). DPO training effective.\"\n112\t    priority: null\n113\t    registry_id: diag-lora-merge\n114\t    status: done\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"878d988b-bed6-4e7a-992f-4deb455a95a2","uuid":"211928d5-98bf-4b14-b3c8-30fdb990249b","timestamp":"2026-05-13T15:32:59.571Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/demo_calibrated_sim/experiment_plan.yaml","content":"# Experiment Plan — demo_calibrated_sim\n# Target: EMNLP 2026 Findings (ARR deadline 2026-05-25)\n# Created: 2026-05-13 (post-MVP, D013/D014)\n# Story: Calibration failure taxonomy + scale-dependent failure severity\n\nclaims:\n  C1:\n    statement: \"BCP produces three systematic failure mechanisms (FM1-FM3) at 7B scale\"\n    evidence_strength: moderate\n    notes: \"FM1/FM3 are capacity-dependent (7B only); FM2 persists weakly at 14B\"\n  C2:\n    statement: \"DPO+BCP interaction produces calibration reversal (FM4)\"\n    evidence_strength: strong\n    notes: \"4 runs × 20 tasks, 10/12 variant×run gap expansion\"\n  C3:\n    statement: \"BCP failure severity is scale-dependent — catastrophic at 7B, selective ignoring at 14B\"\n    evidence_strength: strong\n    notes: \"Safety-efficacy gap: scaling resolves harms but not ineffectiveness\"\n  C4:\n    statement: \"Actionable implications for future calibration research\"\n    evidence_strength: analytical\n    notes: \"Grounded in C1-C3 data; 5 implications including scale-dependency\"\n\nplans:\n  # --- P0: Core experiments (all completed) ---\n\n  - plan_id: plan_001\n    name: \"7B BCP Baseline Evaluation\"\n    claim: C1\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct BCP eval on tau-bench retail (6 variants × 19-20 tasks). Documents FM1 (completion collapse), FM2 (cross-dim interference), FM3 (overcorrection rebound at single-run level).\"\n    priority: P0\n    depends_on: []\n    registry_id: mvp-004\n    status: done\n\n  - plan_id: plan_002\n    name: \"7B DPO Training (v3, Fix B)\"\n    claim: C2\n    description: \"DPO fine-tuning on dialect-calibrated preference pairs. Training acc 0.75-1.0, loss 0.653.\"\n    priority: P0\n    depends_on: []\n    registry_id: dpo-003\n    status: done\n\n  - plan_id: plan_003\n    name: \"7B DPO+BCP Evaluation (merged model)\"\n    claim: [C1, C2]\n    description: \"Evaluate DPO-trained merged model with BCP prompts (4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes). Confirms FM3 rebound (4/4 runs). Documents FM4 reversal (10/12 gap expansion).\"\n    priority: P0\n    depends_on: [plan_002]\n    registry_id: hgate-dpo003-merged\n    status: done\n\n  - plan_id: plan_004\n    name: \"14B BCP Cross-Scale Evaluation\"\n    claim: C3\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct FP16 TP=2 BCP eval (4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes). Result: FM1 NOT replicated (H3 4/4 PASS), FM3 NOT replicated (0/4 runs), FM2 weak. BCP still fails (H2 3/4 FAIL) but via 'selective ignoring' not 'catastrophic collapse'.\"\n    priority: P0\n    depends_on: [plan_001]\n    registry_id: qwen14b-bcp-001\n    status: done\n\n  # --- P1: Statistical robustness (no GPU needed) ---\n\n  - plan_id: plan_005\n    name: \"Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons\"\n    claim: [C1, C2, C3]\n    description: \"Bootstrap confidence intervals for: FM4 reversal direction, FM3 rebound magnitude, 7B-vs-14B completion difference, H2 calibration gap. Computational only.\"\n    priority: P1\n    depends_on: [plan_001, plan_003, plan_004]\n    registry_id: null\n    gpu_estimate: \"0h (CPU only)\"\n    status: planned\n\n  # --- P2: Optional strengthening ---\n\n  - plan_id: plan_006\n    name: \"14B DPO + BCP (scale-dependent FM4?)\"\n    claim: [C2, C3]\n    description: \"DPO training + BCP eval at 14B scale. Tests whether FM4 (DPO+BCP reversal) is also scale-dependent. If 14B DPO+BCP shows no reversal → FM4 is capacity-dependent too. If reversal persists → FM4 is the one truly structural failure.\"\n    priority: P2\n    depends_on: [plan_004]\n    registry_id: null\n    gpu_estimate: \"~11-13h on 2× RTX 4090 (6-8h training + 5h eval)\"\n    status: planned\n    notes: \"Not strictly necessary — C2 already strong at 7B. Interesting for story depth. Contingent on new server GPU availability post-migration.\"\n\n  # --- Auxiliary experiments (supporting data, not primary evidence) ---\n\n  - plan_id: plan_aux_001\n    name: \"Llama-3-8B BCP (floor effect)\"\n    claim: C3\n    description: \"Llama-3-8B completion=5%. Tool-calling insufficient for 16-tool tau-bench. Cannot isolate BCP effect.\"\n    priority: null\n    registry_id: llama-bcp-001\n    status: done\n    notes: \"Not usable for cross-model generalization. Documented in limitations.\"\n\n  - plan_id: plan_aux_002\n    name: \"Mistral-7B BCP (floor effect)\"\n    claim: C3\n    description: \"Mistral-7B completion=0%. Cannot do structured tool_calls. No usable data.\"\n    priority: null\n    registry_id: mistral-bcp-001\n    status: done\n    notes: \"Confirms 16-tool setting is challenging for 7B non-Qwen models.\"\n\n  - plan_id: plan_aux_003\n    name: \"LoRA Merge Diagnostics\"\n    claim: C2\n    description: \"Verified LoRA merge correctness (merged 60% vs base 40% completion). DPO training effective.\"\n    priority: null\n    registry_id: diag-lora-merge\n    status: done\n","numLines":115,"startLine":1,"totalLines":115}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PF7R4BsGHRSjxKq9AuYvRq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the experiment plan. The planned experiments (status: planned) are:\n\n1. **plan_005**: Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons (P1, CPU only)\n2. **plan_006**: 14B DPO + BCP (P2, ~11-13h GPU)\n\nI need to generate detailed imagined data for these two planned experiments under the \"best\" tier. Let me also check if there's any memory or other context files that might be relevant.\n\nLet me now construct the detailed YAML output based on:\n\n**Best tier definition:**\n- Bootstrap CIs confirm all core effects\n\n\n- 14B DPO completed revealing scale-dependent FM4 patterns\n- Taxonomy established as four well-separated mechanisms with statistical backing\n\nI'm setting the key metric ranges for the best-case scenario, with FM4 gap reversals between 10-24 percentage points, FM3 rebounds from 3.5-11.0 points, scale completion differences spanning 15-35 points, and the other core metrics reflecting strong effect sizes across the board.\n\nThe effective taxonomy size holds steady at four mechanisms, with estimated acceptance probability between 65-80 percent. The four-mechanism taxonomy is the main contribution here, with scale-dependent severity as the standout finding. The 14B DPO results either add a fifth row to the table or confirm FM4 as scale-invariant—either way, it's publishable.\n\nNow I'm working through the bootstrap CI approach for plan_005, where I need all confidence intervals to exclude zero for the key findings: FM3 rebound, FM4 reversal direction, the 7B-versus-14B completion gap, and the FM2 politeness shift. That statistical backing across all four mechanisms is what makes this solid.\n\nFor plan_006 with the 14B DPO and BCP combination, the training completes successfully and I'm examining how the FM4 pattern manifests at the larger scale. Now I'm working through the actual results from the FM4 reversal experiment, pulling the politeness gap measurements across different runs and comparing the baseline model against the DPO-trained version to see how the gaps shift. Looking at the Indian English results across the same runs, I'm seeing a mean gap of +0.0632, which is similarly small and suggests the DPO+BCP approach isn't effectively reducing politeness disparities for this dialect either. For the FM4 reversal analysis, I have 80 episodes distributed across the 4 runs, and the directional consistency pattern holds at 10 out of 12 cases.\n\nNow I'm trying to interpret what the 95% confidence interval metric actually represents — the key metric range [10, 24] percentage points seems to indicate the CI bounds for the FM4 gap reversal, though I'm working through whether this represents the full interval or something else. Looking at the metric names more carefully, fm4_gap_reversal_95ci_pp with the range [10, 24] appears to be the 95% confidence interval bounds for the FM4 gap reversal measured in percentage points. The h2_best_gap_reduction_pct metric showing [24, 28] represents the best gap reduction achieved from the baseline model, which aligns with what the paper framing indicates about the performance improvements. ]pp — entirely negative, excludes zero ✓\n\nThese confidence intervals represent the best-case scenario results. Now I need to construct the detailed imagined outcomes.\n\nBut wait, there's a discrepancy here. The observed FM4 gap only shifts about +6.4pp per variant on average, so how could the CI span [10, 24]pp? That suggests they're either measuring the reversal magnitude differently or pooling across both variants somehow.\n\nLet me look at the actual gap_change values from the data. Across the four runs, I'm seeing AAVE shifts ranging from -48.7pp down to +5.9pp, and Indian shifts from -37.3pp to +6.8pp. These gap_change values represent...\n\nActually, I'm overcomplicating this. The tier definitions give me the CI ranges I need to use, so let me just focus on generating the best-tier output with those numbers. The core question is understanding what the gap reversal actually measures — it's comparing the politeness gaps under DPO+BCP versus the baseline BCP gaps, and I need to align my imagined results with those reference values from the paper.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers: the base BCP model shows an AAVE gap of -0.091 in the correct direction, but after DPO+BCP training it flips to +0.064, which is a reversal of about 15.5 percentage points. The same pattern holds for Indian English at around 16.2pp. So a mean reversal magnitude of roughly 15.8pp makes sense, and the CI of [10, 24]pp centered around that range with zero entirely excluded confirms this is a real, significant effect. The FM3 rebound at +7.2pp with CI [3.5, 11.0] follows the same logic.\n\nNow looking at the scale completion differences: the 14B model reaches 61.6% while the 7B only gets to 36.8%, a gap of about 24.8pp that aligns with the CI of [15, 35]. For the politeness shift in FM2, the 7B model shows a -14.3pp drop in absolute terms when optimizing for question rate, though the paper reports this as -25.6% in relative terms, which would make the CI of [-32, -19]pp sensible if it's also in relative percentage terms.\n\nI think I have enough to piece together the YAML structure now. For the 14B DPO experiment, the key question is whether FM4 replicates the reversal pattern across scales or whether it gets attenuated or disappears entirely.\n\nRe-reading the narrative, \"reveals scale-dependent FM4 pattern\" really points toward FM4 being attenuated at 14B rather than fully replicated—that would make the strongest case for C3 since it shows all failure modes exhibit scale-dependent behavior. So I'm picturing 14B DPO training completing in about 12 hours on GPU with similar quality metrics to the 7B run (accuracy around 0.75-0.85, loss around 0.65-0.66), then the evaluation running 4 times across 6 variants on 20 tasks to see FM4 reversal show up as attenuated at the larger scale.\n\nLooking at what the numbers might show: only 3-5 out of 12 variant-run checks would show expansion versus 10 out of 12 at 7B, with the gap reversal dropping to maybe +1-3 percentage points instead of the +6.4pp we saw at 7B, possibly even flattening out. The model would continue that selective ignoring pattern—still not applying BCP but without actively reversing it—while completion rates stay robust around 60% or higher. Now I'm structuring the YAML output with the tier designation, narrative guidance, and the imagined experiments that would support this finding.\n\nGenerating the bootstrap CI results... Both model scales show consistent patterns within themselves, though the 14B variant completes the scale task substantially better than 7B by about 25 percentage points. At 7B, the politeness shift on single-dimension questions is dramatic—a 14 point drop from baseline—which is a large enough effect that confidence intervals should exclude zero despite the small sample size. For the 14B DPO+BCP configuration, the FM4 reversal weakens because the base model exhibits selective resistance to the BCP perturbation, so even though DPO training should work effectively at this scale, the interaction between DPO and BCP becomes attenuated when the model fundamentally resists the calibration signal. before and after DPO, so there's less \"interaction\" to produce reversal.\n\nSpecific predicted numbers for 14B DPO+BCP:\n- DPO training: acc 0.72-0.90, loss ~0.655 (slightly worse than 7B due to larger model, but still good)\n- Completion: ~58-65% (similar to base 14B, DPO doesn't harm at this scale)\n- FM4 reversal: attenuated — mean gap expansion only 2-4/12 variant×run checks (vs 10/12 at 7B)\n  - AAVE politeness gap: +0.01 to +0.03 (vs +0.064 at 7B) — barely reversed, near zero\n  - Indian gap: similar\n  - H2 still fails but gap direction is more ambiguous\n\nAt 14B, FM3 follows the same pattern as BCP alone—no catastrophic collapse and no recovery either. This suggests FM4 is also capacity-dependent; the larger model's inherent robustness to BCP prevents DPO from triggering the interaction effect that causes reversal at 7B. So the overarching narrative is that all four failure modes scale differently: 7B shows dramatic breakdowns across FM1, FM3, and FM4, while 14B exhibits a quieter form of failure where calibration issues persist but manifest less visibly. Now I need to generate the complete YAML specification and then think through the tables and figures—particularly a bootstrap confidence interval summary table for key comparisons and a directional consistency matrix showing FM4 results across variants and runs. with bootstrap p-values\n\nTables for plan_006:\n1. 14B DPO training metrics table\n2. 14B DPO+BCP vs 14B BCP comparison table (analogous to existing 7B comparison)\n3. Cross-scale FM replication matrix: expanded to include FM4 at both scales\n\nFigures for plan_005:\n1. Bootstrap distribution histograms for key comparisons\n2. Forest plot of all CIs\n\nFigures for plan_006:\n1. Scale-dependent failure severity heatmap (updated with FM4 row)\n2. Gap reversal visualization at both scales\n3. Completion retention comparison across all conditions\n\nLet me finalize this with one comprehensive YAML block and work through the precise numbers more carefully.\n\nFor FM4's gap reversal, I'm looking at the bootstrap confidence intervals: the AAVE and Indian gaps both reversed by roughly 15-16 percentage points when moving from base BCP to DPO+BCP, with the 95% CI solidly in the [10.2, 21.8]pp range and well clear of zero. The run-to-run variation explains some of the spread, particularly that anomalous run 4 result. For FM3, the rebound effect shows consistent overcorrection in politeness across the runs, averaging around 7.2pp with individual variation across the different experimental conditions. Looking at the bootstrap confidence interval of 3.8 to 10.6 percentage points, which excludes zero and is based on resampling 80 episodes across the four runs — the lower bound is pulled down by runs 2 and 3 sitting around 4pp, but even in the worst case it doesn't cross zero. Now I'm trying to figure out which baseline completion rates to use for the 7B versus 14B comparison, since there are different model variants with different baselines.\n\nThe most direct comparison looks like qwen14b-bcp-001 at 61.6% versus mvp-004 at 36.8%, giving a 24.8 percentage point difference with a bootstrap CI of 16.4 to 33.2pp. For the FM2 politeness shift, I'm seeing a 14.3 percentage point absolute decrease in the single_dim_question condition compared to baseline, though the confidence interval is expressed in relative terms rather than absolute. Looking at the fm2 metric differently — it might be pooling interference effects across dimensions and conditions, so I'll just work with the given ranges. For the 14B DPO+BCP plan, I'm setting up training with Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct using the same 1434 DPO pairs, expecting slightly lower accuracy than the 7B version since the larger model starts closer to correct behavior, though it should still learn effectively from the training signal.\n\nFor the evaluation across 480 episodes, I'm predicting completion rates around 57-63% with a mean of ~59%, since the 14B base already sits at 61.6% and DPO's gains plateau at this scale. On the fairness metrics, I'm seeing an attenuated reversal pattern — the AAVE politeness gap shrinks to just +0.015 compared to +0.064 at 7B, the Indian gap stays around +0.022, and the H2 gap reduction becomes much smaller at -3% instead of -10.3%, suggesting the larger model is more resistant to the debiasing effect.\n\nThe core insight is that at 14B, the model's \"selective ignoring\" of BCP instructions limits how much DPO can exploit the interaction surface — the alignment shifts internal representations but the BCP prompts don't propagate deeply enough to trigger the reversal cascade we see at 7B. FM1 and FM3 still don't replicate, with FM1 maintaining >90% completion retention and FM3 showing overcorrection without the rebound effect. This points to a key finding: the FM4 reversal is fundamentally scale-dependent, emerging from a 7B-specific dynamic where limited model capacity creates tension between DPO alignment and BCP instructions, but at 14B the model has enough capacity to handle both without that tug-of-war.\n\nThis reframes the entire taxonomy — all four failure modes show capacity-dependent severity at different thresholds rather than just three being capacity-dependent. FM1 and FM3 disappear entirely at 14B, FM2 weakens from strong to weak interference, and FM4 attenuates from full reversal down to partial. Now I'm compiling this into the final scale-dependency table and then moving to the YAML writeup.\n\nFor the metric ranges, I'm calibrating the confidence intervals and point estimates to stay within the observed bounds while reflecting the actual data: the gap reversal effect sits around 10-22 percentage points, the rebound effect around 4-11, the completion difference around 16-33, and the non-target shift around -30 to -21. The best gap reduction observed was 27.6%, and the overall acceptance rate should land in the 65-80% range with four distinct mechanisms driving the outcomes.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML documentation with supporting tables. For plan_005, I'll include a bootstrap confidence interval summary and sign-test results broken down by variant and run. For plan_006, I'm adding the 14B DPO training metrics, per-run results with BCP, an updated cross-scale FM replication matrix that now incorporates the 14B FM4 data, and a comparison table showing how the FM4 reversal magnitude attenuates between the 7B and 14B scales.\n\nWriting the YAML structure... Original framing at full strength. Bootstrap CIs statistically validate all four failure mechanisms; 14B DPO reveals FM4 reversal is also scale-dependent (attenuated from 10/12 to 4/12 directional consistency). Taxonomy stands as four well-separated mechanisms — all showing capacity-dependent severity — with C3 (scale-dependent failure severity) as the highlight contribution. Title 1 works. Claims need no hedging beyond standard single-family limitation. Paper story: \"every calibration failure we document is worse at 7B than 14B, yet none disappears — scaling changes how calibration fails, not whether it fails.\"\n\nFor the imagined experiments, I'm planning a non-parametric bootstrap analysis with 10,000 resamples across five key statistics: the FM4 DPO+BCP gap reversal, FM3 overcorrection rebound, 7B-vs-14B completion differences, FM2 politeness shifts, and FM4 directional consistency. I'll compute bias-corrected accelerated confidence intervals for each, expecting FM4 reversal around 15.8 percentage points with a 95% CI of [10.2, 21.8], a sign test p-value of 0.006, and directional consistency dropping from 10/12 at 7B to 4/12 at 14B. I'm noticing the units need adjustment—that nontarget shift should be in percentage points rather than percent. Looking at the FM4 reversal results, the confidence interval excludes zero with a substantial swing from the baseline, and this pattern holds consistently across most runs, though run 4 shows some variation in the AAVE condition.\n\nFor FM3, the rebound effect is similarly robust—all runs demonstrate overcorrection above baseline, and even the smallest effect is far enough from zero that the confidence interval remains positive. The scale completion gap between the 14B and 7B models is dramatic, and while within-model variance creates a wider confidence interval, the effect size is so large that it dominates any overlap concerns.\n\nNow looking at FM2's politeness shift, the confidence interval is quite tight because the effect is substantial—roughly a 25.6% relative drop per episode—and with n=20 episodes, the signal-to-noise ratio is strong enough to pin down the estimate. For the question rate arm, I'm noting the absolute versus relative framing: while the paper reports a 52.3% relative reduction, that translates to only a 2.3 percentage point absolute change, which they explicitly flag to address base-rate concerns.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating markdown table... All four core effects clear the statistical bar with 95% confidence intervals excluding zero, with FM4 reversal showing the strongest signal at over 10 percentage points and FM3 rebound the weakest but still solid at 3.8pp. The scale completion difference is particularly robust, and FM2's politeness shift maintains tight confidence intervals despite the small sample size, while the question_rate arm is presented descriptively to acknowledge its limitations honestly. This bootstrap approach transforms the directional consistency arguments into rigorous statistical claims that directly address the power concerns raised earlier. Now I'm looking at a two-phase experiment design combining DPO fine-tuning with scale-dependent FM4 analysis. Now I'm looking at the evaluation results from the 14B model—the DPO training converged in 3 epochs with no tokenizer issues, and the BCP evaluation shows the completion rate at 0.592 with baseline politeness at 0.518. The hypothesis tests are mixed: H1 and H3 pass across all runs while H2 fails completely, and FM4's dialect gaps actually widen at this scale (AAVE +1.8pp, Indian English +2.4pp) with directional consistency dropping significantly compared to the 7B results. Looking at the per-run completion rates and the dialect-specific gaps across the four runs, I'm seeing variability in how the 14B DPO model handles AAVE and Indian English—some runs show improvement while others show degradation. The rationale suggests the 14B model's stronger baseline is actually limiting the learning signal compared to the 7B version, which had more room to improve.\n\nThe FM4 reversal pattern is particularly interesting: the 14B model shows much weaker directional consistency because BCP prompts barely influence its behavior in the first place, so there's no strong signal for DPO to reverse. The model just continues ignoring BCP prompts regardless of training, which explains why the preference pairs have less leverage on this larger model.\n\nLooking at the statistical evidence, the politeness gaps at 14B are small and directionally unclear, with confidence intervals that cross zero — so the reversal isn't statistically significant. But when I compare the reversal magnitude across scales (roughly 15.8pp at 7B versus 2.1pp at 14B), that 13.7pp difference is itself statistically significant, confirming FM4 is genuinely capacity-dependent. This puts FM4 alongside FM1 and FM3 as scale-dependent failure modes, while FM2 remains the only mechanism that shows up at both scales, though even that one is attenuated.\n\nCompletion rates stay around 59.2% at 14B because DPO doesn't degrade tool-calling — similar to how 7B improved from 40% to 60% with DPO. The 14B baseline is already at 61.6%, so there's less room for improvement, and the slight dip is just noise. H3 passes all four checks.\n\nH2 still fails completely at 14B DPO: the model refuses to use BCP regardless of whether DPO is applied, so calibration remains ineffective. This highlights the core tension in C3 — scaling eliminates the harmful interaction but doesn't actually build calibration.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating markdown table... Generating comparison tables... /12, CI excludes zero)\", \"Attenuated (4/12, CI includes zero)\", \"Capacity-dependent: attenuated\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scale-dependent failure severity heatmap (updated): 4×2 grid (FM1-FM4 × 7B/14B), color-coded by severity (red=catastrophic, orange=moderate, yellow=weak, green=absent). At 7B, FM1/FM3/FM4 are red, FM2 orange. At 14B, all are green or yellow. Visual demonstrates that scaling systematically reduces failure severity without achieving calibration.\"\n      - \"FM4 reversal comparison panel: side-by-side violin plots of politeness gap distributions at 7B and 14B. 7B distribution clearly shifted positive (reversed); 14B distribution centered near zero with wide spread. Horizontal dashed line at zero demonstrates attenuation visually.\"\n      - \"Gap trajectory plot: x-axis = model scale (7B, 14B), y-axis = mean politeness gap (pp). Separate lines for base BCP and DPO+BCP. Base BCP shows negative gaps (correct direction) at both scales. DPO+BCP shows strong positive gap (reversal) at 7B, near-zero at 14B. Lines converge at 14B, illustrating that DPO+BCP interaction diminishes with scale.\"\n    analysis: The 14B DPO result is the strongest outcome for the paper because it establishes FM4 as scale-dependent, making all four failure modes capacity-dependent at different thresholds and transforming the findings from partial to comprehensive.\n\nThe core narrative sharpens considerably: all four calibration failures are more severe at smaller scales, but none fully disappear—they shift from catastrophic at 7B to silent at 14B. Scaling proves necessary for safety but insufficient for calibration. This unified pattern is stronger than the current framing of three confirmed scale-dependent modes plus one uncertain.\n\nFor reviewers, the 14B DPO data directly addresses whether larger models would resolve the issue, preempting a major objection and strengthening the paper's claims about the fundamental nature of these failures.\n\nWith both experimental plans completed, I'm estimating 70-75% acceptance probability for EMNLP Findings, anchored on comprehensive statistical rigor, the clean four-mechanism taxonomy with consistent scale-dependency, and full coverage across two calibration paradigms and two model scales. The main vulnerability remains the single model family, though the depth of analysis within Qwen2.5 should mitigate reviewer concerns.\n\nNow I'm double-checking the per-run AAVE gaps for the