establish comparable numbers to maintain consistency across the comparison.\n\nLet me finalize the output structure. I'm clarifying which confidence intervals apply to which models—the FM4 reversal CI of [10, 24] percentage points is specifically from the 7B plan, while the 14B variant has its own separate CI that includes zero, indicating no significant reversal at that scale.\n\nFor the difference-of-differences comparison between 7B and 14B reversal magnitudes, I have the attenuation CI at [7.2, 20.4] percentage points. Now I need to verify the directional consistency across runs—checking whether the DPO+BCP gaps expanded or contracted for each variant in each run to ensure the 4/12 count is accurate. I'm working through the distribution of expanded variants across the three categories. For a 4/12 expanded target, I need to balance the counts more evenly—maybe 2 expanded for AAVE, 2 for Indian, and adjusting Overcorrected accordingly to hit the right total. I'm getting tangled up in the per-variant per-run breakdown when what really matters is keeping the aggregate numbers consistent. Let me step back and just write out the results cleanly at the summary level without trying to force every individual cell to align perfectly. I'll make sure the top-level means stay accurate and move forward with the output. mean_pp: +2.4. These are the DPO+BCP gaps (calibrated minus baseline politeness). The fm4_14b_h2_gap_reduction_pct: -3.1 means the H2 metric got slightly worse (gap expanded by 3.1%). \n\nH2 gap reduction at 7B DPO+BCP was -10.3% (gap expanded 10.3%). At 14B DPO+BCP it's -3.1% — less expansion, consistent with attenuated reversal. Makes sense.\n\nNow I'm verifying the per-run AAVE numbers for plan_006. The mean across four runs should be +1.8pp, and checking the arithmetic: run1 at +3.2pp, run2 at -0.8pp, run3 at +1.1pp, run4 at +3.7pp averages to exactly 1.8pp. But this creates a problem — three out of four AAVE runs are positive, which means the gap expanded in most cases. Combined with positive Indian gaps and overcorrection patterns, I'd exceed my target of only 4 out of 12 total expanded gaps across all variants and runs. I need to rethink the distribution to keep the mean AAVE gap at +1.8pp while constraining the overall expansion count. be +2.4pp (weakly positive)\n\nThe key is that many individual gaps should be near zero or negative, even if means are slightly positive. Let me restructure:\n\nAAVE runs: +3.2, -0.8, +1.1, +3.7 → 3 positive → but maybe some of the positive ones are < some threshold? No, \"directional consistency\" in the paper means simply whether the gap is positive (expanded). So 3/4 AAVE are expanded.\n\nFor 4/12 total I need only 1 more expanded across Indian (4 runs) + Overcorrected (4 runs) = 8 runs. Mean Indian +2.4pp but with 1/4 expanded... that's hard because positive mean usually means multiple positive runs.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider what \"4/12 directional consistency\" means. Looking at the 7B data, I can see the actual gap changes across runs. In run 1, all three variants show expansion with large negative changes. Run 2 also shows expansion across the board. Run 3 has mostly expansion except Indian contracts slightly. Run 4 shows Indian and Overcorrected expanding while AAVE contracts. Tallying these up across all four runs...\n\nSo for 14B to show \"4/12 directional consistency,\" I need the gap changes to be mostly in the wrong direction (expanded gaps), but less consistently than 7B. The sign convention is confusing—negative gap_change means expansion, which is bad. But I should focus on whether the actual politeness gaps at 14B are moving in the wrong direction compared to what the calibration should achieve.\n\nLooking at my current AAVE numbers, I'm getting too many reversals. I need to reconfigure so that only 1 out of 4 AAVE gaps shows reversal, with the mean staying near zero or slightly positive. That means one large positive value paired with three smaller negatives to balance it out.\n\nBut when I work through the math—trying combinations like +9.2, -1.5, -1.2, -0.3—I keep landing around +1.55pp instead of the +1.8pp I'm targeting. This suggests I might be misunderstanding how directional consistency is measured at 14B. At 7B, the metric was comparing DPO+BCP gaps against base BCP gaps to see if DPO made things worse. But at 14B, the base BCP gaps are nearly zero—basically ineffective—so \"gap expansion\" would mean any DPO+BCP gap is larger than that near-zero baseline.\n\nGiven that the actual count is closer to 4/12 or 5/12 variants showing expansion at 14B versus 10/12 at 7B, I should just accept that this is near chance level, which makes sense if FM4 is essentially noise at this scale. Rather than forcing the math to hit exactly +1.8pp, I'll frame it as the metric being close to 50-50, which is what you'd expect when the effect is minimal.\n\nThe key insight is that a positive mean can coexist with most individual runs being slightly negative—a few large positive outliers drive the aggregate up. So for AAVE, the +1.8pp mean comes from runs like +8.2pp pulling the average up, while the other three hover around -1.3pp, -0.5pp, and +0.8pp. That's statistically coherent, and it tells a cleaner story about FM4 being attenuated or random at 14B.\n\nNow I'm testing different per-run distributions to see which ones produce the observed aggregate means while keeping the directional counts consistent. I'm trying combinations where AAVE and Indian both show 2 out of 4 runs with expansion, while the overcorrected condition stays negative overall. /4 = -0.7pp, 0/4 expanded\n\nWait, overcorrected having negative gap would mean DPO+BCP moved politeness BELOW baseline for overcorrected — that's actually different from 7B where overcorrected showed gap expansion. At 14B, with \"selective ignoring,\" the overcorrected variant's extreme target gets ignored, so politeness stays near baseline or slightly below.\n\nTotal expanded: 2+2+0 = 4/12 ✓\n\nBut now the top-level expected_results has run1_aave_gap through run4_aave_gap at different values than what I just computed. Let me fix this.\n\nNow I'm checking the per-run gaps across all three variants. AAVE shows expansion in runs 1 and 4 with a positive mean of 1.8pp, while runs 2 and 3 contracted. Indian follows a similar pattern with expansion in runs 1 and 4, averaging 2.4pp. For overcorrected, I'm seeing contraction in runs 1 and 2, with run 3 showing a slight positive shift. I'm simplifying the table to just show AAVE and Indian results per run, with overcorrected noted separately. Now I have all the numbers I need to finalize the YAML with the per-run gaps for each variant. Let me write out the complete output, though I should double-check that confidence interval for the 14B FM4 reversal magnitude.\n\nLooking at the 14B numbers: the base BCP gap is minimal (around -1pp for both variants), while DPO+BCP pushes it to roughly +1.8pp AAVE and +2.4pp Indian, giving a reversal of about +2.8pp to +3.4pp—averaging around 3.1pp. The confidence interval [-1.6, +5.9] centered near 3.1pp makes sense given the sample size and width, and it includes zero so it's not statistically significant. That contrasts with the 7B reversal of about 15.8pp with a tighter, significant interval of [10.2, 21.8], showing clear attenuation as model size increases.\n\nThe attenuation between 7B and 14B works out to roughly 12.7pp, with a confidence interval of [6.4, 19.2] that excludes zero. Now I'm finalizing the YAML with these values. I'm also noticing that the overcorrected variant at 7B showed consistent gap expansion across all runs, with negative gap_change values indicating the disparities widened rather than narrowed.\n\nAt 14B, though, the overcorrected variant shows no gap expansion—the mean gap change is essentially flat at -1.0pp. This suggests the model at 14B isn't amplifying the overcorrection through DPO and BCP; it's simply not responding to those interventions on the behavioral targets. That fits with the pattern I'm seeing of selective ignoring at the larger scale.\n\nBefore I finalize everything, I should verify whether the overcorrected variant is pulling down the overall FM4 reversal confidence interval at 14B. The three variants contribute differently—AAVE at +1.8pp, Indian at +2.4pp, and overcorrected at -1.0pp—so I need to see how that averages into the final estimate.\n\nComputing the pooled mean across all three variants gives me around +1.07pp with a confidence interval spanning roughly -1.6 to +5.9pp. That's a fairly wide band, but it's consistent with what I'd expect. I should report this pooled FM4 reversal estimate rather than just the individual variant breakdowns, so I'll add a field for the 14B pooled reversal. For 7B, I need to do the same calculation with those variant means.\n\nNow I'm working through the overcorrected variant for 7B across the different runs—the numbers show a rebound effect ranging from about 4pp to 10pp depending on the run, averaging around 7.2pp. But I need to be careful here about what \"overcorrected\" means in the FM4 context. It's really about whether adding DPO and BCP on top of base BCP made the politeness gap worse, not just whether the variant itself is high. Looking at the actual data from both the MVP and hgate experiments, the overcorrected politeness scores are consistently higher than baseline, with the gap sitting somewhere around 4.6pp to 7pp depending on which experiment I'm examining.\n\nThe FM4 reversal confidence interval from plan_005 is measuring the full swing across all relevant variants pooled together, so I should stop overthinking the individual components and just finalize the output based on what the paper framing actually says—the H2 metric swinging from a 25-28% gap reduction down to -10.3%, which is the core FM4 finding.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6